  "surprise": "Discard: 623/607/182 (exact mean 470.67) vs 153-claude's 676/700/685 (687.00): -216.33, paired -53/-93/-503, every seed down and far below the registered -35..+35 range. The registered sampler falsifier fired on every seed: round-1 RL targets solved 651/276/86 (153: 766/747/694), round-1 sample acc 0.178/0.056/0.015 (153 seed 0: 0.271), and 21,114/29,688/31,374 of the 48,000 round-1 samples failed to parse (44/62/65%). Seeds 0/1 recovered to 4-6k parse failures by round 2 but stayed behind (round-4 solved 1112/1030 vs 1157/1168/1191); seed 2 never recovered (parse-fail 29,075/25,396/24,371 in rounds 2-4 and 36,611 of 70,912 at eval, round-4 solved 428, eval 182 with nothing above 10 lines). Pretraining itself landed inside or better than every registered band: n_params 9,557,760; steps 4477/4435/4456 (153: 4540/4479/4489, -1.5%; registered 4,400-4,750, so the per-head reshapes were free as predicted); epochs 3.96/3.92/3.94; final val 0.0826/0.0874/0.0790 (153: 0.088/0.087/0.089), min val 0.0793/0.0725/0.0779; last train CE 0.046/0.080/0.033 (153: 0.060); held-out greedy 0.9475/0.959/0.9605 (153: 0.9675; seed 0 below the 0.955 band, the only pretraining-side miss). RL sampling 211-357 samples/s, totals 1795/1620/1469 s. So this is the 159-fable/167-claude/168-fable3/169-fable pattern in its strongest form yet: an attention-side change that keeps greedy decoding (eval greedy 0.983-0.9875) and matches or lowers the loss, but wrecks T 0.8 sampling; 167-claude's 34-50% round-1 parse failures cost -13, here 44-65% cost -53 to -503, and the seed-to-seed spread (sd 250 vs the usual ~25) says the damage is a threshold effect, not a shift. Interpretation, not measured: per-head orthogonalization hands every head a full-norm rank-48 update every step, so a head whose gradient is near zero (unused, or already fitted) receives pure noise at the full Muon step under only 0.1 x lr per-step decay; whole-matrix orthogonalization implicitly gates such heads because their rows get a small share of the shared singular vectors. Noisy q/k in a few heads are harmless under greedy decoding, where the confident heads dominate, but once the sampler takes an off-greedy token the state is one where those heads inject garbage, and the proof stops parsing. Claims (1)-(2) (the per-head maps are the modular dual) are untested by this; claim (3), that equal per-head steps help the sampler, is falsified. Do not retry head-wise Muon without gating each head's update by its momentum norm (or a per-head trust ratio), and treat any change that raises round-1 parse failures above ~10k as a sampler regression regardless of loss.",
